+# AreaComponents
+
+Areas as **components** instead of as object classes: a *shape* component that owns the geometry,
+and one or more *function* components on the same entity that give that geometry a meaning — an
+audio/gameplay area, a proximity trigger, a tube of gravity, a row of meshes along a curve.
+
+Everything lives in the Sandbox category **Area**, both in **Create Object → Area** and in
+**Add Component → Area**.
+
+This is **additive**. The engine's own Area objects (AreaBox, AreaSphere, AreaShape,
+SplineDistributor, GravityVolume and the rest) are untouched: same classes, same level XML, same
+behaviour, and they coexist with these components in one level. There is no converter and nothing
+to migrate. Vis areas, portals and occluders are deliberately **not** covered — they are level
+structure baked into the level's indoor data and are static by design.
+
+Part of **ReC Sandbox — a sandbox for recording work**, by Sherefox.
+
+---
+
+## Install / deploy
+
+Three binaries, plus one line in the project file.
+
+| File | Destination |
+| --- | --- |
+| `AreaComponents.dll` | the engine's `bin/win_x64/` **and** the project's `bin/win_x64/` |
+| `AreaComponentsEditor.dll` | the engine's `bin/win_x64/EditorPlugins/` |
+| `Sandbox.exe`, `EditorCommon.dll`, `MFCToolsPlugin.dll`, `CryQt.dll` | the engine's `bin/win_x64/` |
+
+`AreaComponents.dll` goes to **both** bins on purpose: the editor loads the engine copy, the
+launcher loads the project copy.
+
+The Sandbox binaries are part of the install because the editor core itself gained the generic
+hook this feature needs: any component may now tell the editor about geometry it owns — bounds,
+picking, and a row of buttons per component that start an edit tool. Without the rebuilt Sandbox
+the components still load, but there is no **Edit Shape** button, clicking an outline does not
+select the entity, and **F** frames a point instead of a volume. The hook is inert for every entity
+that carries no such component, so nothing else in the editor changes.
+
+Then add the plugin to the project's `.cryproject`, in the `plugins` list beside the other native
+plugins:
+
+```json
+{ "guid": "", "type": "EType::Native", "path": "bin/win_x64/AreaComponents.dll" }
+```
+
+`CryEntitySystem.dll` and `CryDefaultEntities.dll` are also part of this build (the area runtime
+gained an authored fade distance, a geometry-move entry point, and a fix that stopped an area
+entity moving itself). Deploy those two alongside if you are installing from source.
+
+---
+
+## The idea: one shape, several functions
+
+**Shape components** are pure geometry — points, a radius, a size, a curve — in the entity's own
+space. There are four kinds:
+
+| Component | What it is |
+| --- | --- |
+| **Shape: Box** | an axis-aligned box |
+| **Shape: Sphere** | a sphere |
+| **Shape: Polygon** | a footprint of points, optionally extruded in Z |
+| **Shape: Spline** | a Bezier curve — the path form of a shape |
+
+**Exactly one shape per entity.** The four kinds declare each other incompatible, so once an entity
+carries one, the other three are greyed out in **Add Component** with a tooltip saying why. Child
+entities are how you build something that needs two.
+
+**Function components** sit on the same entity and take their volume or curve from whichever shape
+is there. They never ask which kind it is; they ask the shape.
+
+| Component | What it does | Needs |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| **Area** | enter / leave / near / fade events to linked entities — the semantics of a legacy area object | box, sphere or polygon |
+| **Trigger Bounds** | feeds the shape's bounds to the entity's proximity trigger | any shape |
+| **Gravity Volume** | a tube of physics gravity around the curve | spline |
+| **Distributor** | places meshes along the curve | spline |
+
+Add a function component *before* its shape and it binds on the next entity move or property edit
+rather than instantly — there is no "a component was added" event to hang it on. The Create presets
+below add both at once, so this only shows up when building an entity by hand.
+
+Because the function is separate from the geometry, combinations the old objects could not make are
+now just two components on one entity: a distributor on the same spline that drives a gravity
+volume, an audio **Environment** component beside an **Area**, and so on.
+
+---
+
+## Creating
+
+**Create Object → Area** has four entries:
+
+| Entry | Gesture | What you get |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| **Box** | press, drag out a rectangle on the ground, release | Shape: Box (the rectangle is the footprint, the box stands on the ground, 2 m tall by default) + **Area** |
+| **Sphere** | press at the centre, drag out the radius, release | Shape: Sphere + **Area** |
+| **Polygon** | click each point, **double-click** or **Enter** to finish | Shape: Polygon + **Area** |
+| **Spline** | click each point, **double-click** or **Enter** to finish | Shape: Spline (no function — pick one) |
+
+A click with no drag leaves the kind's default size. **Esc** during the gesture discards the whole
+thing; so does finishing a polygon with fewer than three points, or a spline with fewer than two.
+The whole creation is **one undo step**, and the new entity ends up selected.
+
+For anything else, **Add Component → Area** carries the same four shapes and the four functions, so
+a shape can be added to an entity that already exists and a function can be added to a shape that
+has none.
+
+---
+
+## Editing a shape
+
+Select the entity and press **Edit Shape** on the shape component in the inspector. Yellow handles
+appear on the shape's points — every point of a polygon or spline, two opposite corners of a box,
+the centre and one radius handle of a sphere. One tool edits all four; it does not know which kind
+it has.
+
+| Gesture | Effect |
+| --- | --- |
+| click a handle | select it (click away to deselect) |
+| drag | move the point — freely on the construction plane, or along an axis with the move gizmo |
+| **Ctrl**+hover an edge | the edge highlights (on a spline the highlight follows the **curve**) |
+| **Ctrl**+click an edge | insert a point there |
+| **Delete**, or double-click a point | remove the selected point |
+| **Ctrl+Shift**+click | snap the selected point down onto terrain or geometry |
+| **Esc** | leave the tool |
+
+Snapping follows the editor's own settings: grid snap on every drag, terrain riding while
+snap-to-terrain is on. A removal that would take a closed polygon below three points, or a spline
+below two, is refused with a line in the console rather than silently allowed.
+
+**One gesture is one undo step** — each drag, each insert and each delete steps back on its own,
+and the creation itself undoes in one.
+
+Outside the tool: clicking an **outline** (an edge, a polygon's wall, a sphere's surface, the curve)
+selects the entity, and **F** frames the shape at its current size — including right after a size
+typed into the inspector. Shapes draw in the engine's area blue and in the pulsing magenta of a
+selection, with sound-obstructing edges in the highlight orange, so a component shape reads the same
+as a legacy shape standing next to it.
+
+Polygon and Spline also carry a **Recenter Pivot** button: it moves the entity's pivot onto the
+points' centroid and rewrites the points so that nothing moves in the world — the point numbers
+simply become readable. One undo step.
+
+---
+
+## The shape components
+
+### Shape: Box
+
+| Property | Default | Meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| **Size** | (1, 1, 1) | Full size of the box in metres. |
+| **Offset** | (0, 0, 0) | Translation of the box centre in the entity's local space. |
+
+Entity scale is honoured exactly, including a non-uniform one: a scaled axis-aligned box is still a
+box.
+
+### Shape: Sphere
+
+| Property | Default | Meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| **Radius** | 1 | Radius of the sphere in metres. |
+| **Offset** | (0, 0, 0) | Translation of the sphere centre in the entity's local space. |
+
+A sphere cannot represent a non-uniform entity scale — that would be an ellipsoid — so it takes the
+**largest axis** of the scale and warns once in the console, naming the entity and telling you to
+author the size with **Radius** instead. Moving the entity re-arms the warning, so a *new*
+non-uniform scale is reported again. A uniform scale is honoured silently.
+
+### Shape: Polygon
+
+| Property | Default | Meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| **Height** | 0 | Extrusion of the footprint in metres. **0 means infinite in Z** — the same thing a legacy shape's height of 0 means. |
+| **Closed** | true | Whether the last point connects back to the first. An open polygon is a polyline with no interior. |
+| **Obstruct Roof** | false | Whether the top cap obstructs sound. |
+| **Obstruct Floor** | false | Whether the bottom cap obstructs sound. |
+| **Offset** | (0, 0, 0) | Translation of the whole polygon in the entity's local space. |
+| **Points** | — | The point list, in the shape's local space. |
+
+Each point row carries **Position** and **Obstruct Sound**. The flag belongs to the edge that
+*starts* at that point, which is the legacy `Side:N` ordering; keeping it on the point means an
+insert or a delete can never desync it from the geometry.
+
+The extrusion band's floor is the **lowest point's Z**, as in the engine's own area shapes.
+
+### Shape: Spline
+
+| Property | Default | Meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| **Closed** | false | Whether the last point connects back to the first, closing the curve **smoothly** — no corner at point 0, which the legacy spline could not do. |
+| **Offset** | (0, 0, 0) | Translation of the whole spline in the entity's local space. |
+| **Smooth Iterations** | 0 | Rounds the corners of the control polygon before the curve is fitted, 0–4 passes. Every authored point stays **on** the curve and stays draggable. 0 is the legacy curve exactly. |
+| **Tension** | 1 | How far the curve bulges between its points, 0–1. 1 is the legacy handle length exactly; 0 makes the curve the straight control polygon. |
+| **Frame Mode** | Legacy Up | How the normal that orients roads and distributed meshes is built along the curve. **Legacy Up** is the engine's own rule (the cross product with world up): fine while the curve is roughly horizontal, and it swings through half a turn as the curve passes vertical. **Parallel Transport** carries one normal along the curve instead, so a near-vertical stretch stops spinning what rides on it. It is seeded from the legacy normal, so a flat spline is identical either way. |
+| **Points** | — | The point list, in the shape's local space. |
+
+Each point row carries **Position**, **Angle** (roll about the tangent in degrees — what banks a
+road and rolls a distributed instance), **Default Width** (use the consumer's own width here) and,
+when that is off, an explicit **Width** in metres, plus **Obstruct Sound** for the segment that
+starts at the point. The two Bezier handles are derived, not stored.
+
+---
+
+## The function components
+
+### Area
+
+The semantics that used to be baked into every area object, as one component: an id, a group, a
+priority, a near band, an inner fade, and the entities the events go to.
+
+| Property | Default | Meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| **Area Id** | 0 | Id handed to the linked entities with every area event. |
+| **Group** | -1 | Areas sharing a group are exclusive — an entity is only ever in the innermost one. -1 means no group. |
+| **Priority** | 0 | Which area of the same group wins where they overlap. Higher wins. |
+| **Fade Distance** | 0 | Width in metres of the near band **outside** the shape, in which entities get near/fade events. 0 disables it. |
+| **Inner Fade Distance** | 0 | Width in metres **inside** the shape over which a lower-priority area of the same group fades out. |
+| **Link Name** | (empty) | Which of the entity's links this area sends its events to. Empty means every link. |
+| **Linked** | — | Read-only: `3 linked`, or `2 linked, 1 not loaded`, or `none - link entities to this one with the Link tool`. |
+
+**Links are the entity's own links.** This component keeps no list of its own: pick the area with
+Sandbox's **Link tool** and click the target, or use the entity's **Links** section in the
+inspector. The read-only **Linked** row is derived from that and follows it immediately, undo
+included.
+
+**Fade Distance is authored here**, which a legacy area could not do: its near band used to be
+whatever the widest *linked audio entity* asked for, so an area with nothing linked had no band at
+all. It is now the larger of the two. Type `es_DrawAreas 1` in the console to watch the band grow
+and shrink as you change the number.
+
+Audio works unchanged: put the engine's **Environment** or **Area** audio components on the same
+entity and they receive exactly what they would on a legacy area object, because this component
+attaches its host entity to its own area the way the old object did.
+
+Roof and floor sound obstruction come from the **shape**, not from here.
+
+### Trigger Bounds
+
+Feeds the shape's bounds to the entity's proximity trigger, so entities crossing it fire enter and
+leave events. It needed no engine change at all, which is rather the point: the shape contract is
+not the Area component's private property.
+
+| Property | Default | Meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| **Forward Events To** | 0 | Entity id that should receive the enter/leave events instead of this entity. 0 keeps them here. |
+| **Log Events** | false | Print one console line whenever an entity enters or leaves the trigger. Nothing in the editor displays the raw events, so this is how you see it working. |
+
+The component draws its trigger as an **orange box**, and that box is deliberately not the shape's
+outline: a proximity trigger is an axis-aligned box in entity space with rotation and scale ignored,
+so a rotated shape and its trigger genuinely disagree. Drawing the truth beats drawing the
+flattering version.
+
+Entities already standing inside when the level starts are caught, not only later crossings.
+
+**Carry either Trigger Bounds or Area on one entity, never both**: a proximity trigger and an area
+put different things in the same event payload slot.
+
+### Gravity Volume
+
+A tube of physics gravity around the spline. Unlike the legacy gravity volume, all of its parameters
+are saved, and the tube is **rebuilt as soon as the curve changes** — drag a point, let go, and the
+gravity follows in the same step instead of waiting for a level reload.
+
+| Property | Default | Meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| **Enabled** | true | Whether the gravity area exists at all. |
+| **Radius** | 4 | Radius of the tube around the spline, in metres. |
+| **Gravity** | 10 | Strength of the gravity inside the tube, in m/s², along the tube's own up axis. |
+| **Falloff** | 0.8 | Parametric distance (0–1) from the centre at which the gravity starts falling off. |
+| **Damping** | 1 | Uniform damping applied to bodies inside the tube. |
+| **Don't Disable Invisible** | false | Keep the area active while the entity is not rendered. |
+
+What is drawn is what is physicalized — rings around the real curve. In the legacy object the two
+disagreed: it drew one interpolation and simulated another.
+
+### Distributor
+
+Places meshes along the spline. The legacy object had one mesh, one step, follow-on/off and one Z
+angle; this has a weighted list, four spacing modes, five alignments, jitter, ramps, trims and a
+stored seed.
+
+**Meshes** is the list distributed **along** the curve — one row per entry, each with a **Weight**
+(how often it is picked relative to the others) and a **Mesh** (`.cgf`). **End Cap Mesh** is a
+separate, optional mesh placed **once at each end** of an open curve and nowhere in between, so a
+distributor whose only mesh sits there produces exactly two objects — and says so in the console.
+
+| Property | Default | Meaning |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| **Enabled** | true | Whether any instances are placed at all. |
+| **Seed** | 1 | Seed of everything random here. The same seed always gives the same placement. |
+| **Spacing Mode** | Fixed Step | **Fixed Step** (every *Step* metres), **Fixed Count** (*Count* instances over the curve), **Density** (instances per metre), **Mesh Length** (as many sections as the mesh's own length tiles into the curve, rounded, the residual spread over the spacing). |
+| **Step** | 4 | Distance between instances in metres (Fixed Step). |
+| **Count** | 10 | How many instances to place (Fixed Count). |
+| **Density** | 0.25 | Instances per metre (Density). |
+| **Spacing Jitter** | 0 | How far along the curve an instance may wander from its even position, as a fraction of the spacing. |
+| **Trim Start** / **Trim End** | 0 / 0 | Metres of curve left empty at each end. |
+| **Skip Probability** | 0 | Chance (0–1) that any one instance is left out, for a broken-up line. |
+| **Alignment** | Follow Tangent | **Follow Tangent** (the tangent at the instance's own sample), **World Up**, **Surface Normal**, **Fixed**, or **Point To Next** — the instance keeps its position but aims its forward axis at where the *next* instance starts, which is what a straight section (a fence panel, a wall) needs so the chain does not open at every bend. |
+| **Forward Axis** | +X | Which axis of the mesh points along the curve. +X is what the legacy spline distributor assumes, so the same `.cgf` lines up untouched; a mesh modelled sideways needs the dropdown, not a re-export. |
+| **Stretch To Fit** | false | In Point To Next, scale each instance along its forward axis until it reaches the next one, so sections meet with no gap. The stretch is applied in mesh space, so "longer" means longer along the fence, not taller. |
+| **Pivot At Start** | true | Put the **start face** of the mesh's bounding box on the sample point instead of the mesh's own pivot, so a section exported around its centre does not begin half a section early. |
+| **Z Angle** | 0 | A constant turn about the instance's own up axis, in degrees. |
+| **Rotation Jitter** | (0, 0, 0) | Random rotation about each axis, in degrees, plus or minus. |
+| **Scale Min** / **Scale Max** | 1 / 1 | Instance scale range. |
+| **Scale Ramp Start** / **Scale Ramp End** | 1 / 1 | Extra scale multiplier at each end of the curve, linear in between. |
+| **Width Scales** | false | Multiply the instance scale by the spline's own per-point width at that point. |
+| **Default Width** | 1 | The width a point flagged "default width" contributes. |
+| **Offset Min** / **Offset Max** | (0,0,0) / (0,0,0) | Random offset range from the curve, in the instance's own frame. |
+| **Snap To Terrain** | false | Drop every instance onto the terrain height under it. |
+| **Outdoor Only**, **Cast Shadows**, **Rain Occluder**, **Register By BBox**, **Hideable**, **Exclude From Triangulation**, **No Decals**, **Receive Wind**, **Good Occluder**, **View Distance Ratio**, **LOD Ratio** | — | The brush render-flag block, as on any brush. Cast Shadows defaults on; the two ratios default to 100. |
+
+Placement is **deterministic**: one random stream seeded from **Seed** mixed with the instance
+index and drawn in a fixed order, so turning one feature off does not reshuffle everything after it.
+
+Every real rebuild prints **one console line** with the curve length, the spacing mode and its
+numbers, trim, skip, alignment, forward axis, how many meshes were listed and how many actually
+loaded, whether the end cap loaded, how many instances were asked for, how many were placed and how
+many nodes are alive. When something produces nothing, that line says which of those it was. An open
+inspector panel does not cause rebuilds: an unchanged parameter digest returns immediately.
+
+**Bake To Brushes** is a button on the component. It turns the instances into ordinary brush objects
+— one per instance, named `<entity>_baked*`, all inside a single undo step — and then switches the
+distributor's own **Enabled** off. One **Ctrl+Z** removes the brushes and brings the live instances
+back. Two limits, both logged rather than hidden: the baked brushes get a hand-placed brush's
+**default render flags** (the flag block above, the view-distance and LOD ratios are not carried
+over), and they are created flat with a shared name prefix rather than inside a group.
+
+---
+
+## Behaviour worth knowing
+
+**Instances and areas are owned by the entity and are not written into the level.** A distributor's
+meshes are render nodes the component owns; the level file stores the parameters and the seed, and
+the instances are rebuilt from them. The level's object export skips them entirely — adding twenty
+instances does not grow the exported octree data. You still see them on export and in GameLauncher,
+because the entity recreates them there from the same parameters.
+
+**The Area component's hidden area proxy leaves no trace in the level.** It is flagged not to save,
+so an entity carrying Shape + Area writes no `<Area>` node: the geometry exists once, in the shape
+component, and the proxy is rebuilt from it on load. Saving and reloading is the test — the
+components come back, the node was never there.
+
+**Links resolve late, and keep resolving.** A link whose target lives in a layer that was not loaded
+shows as `not loaded` and attaches by itself when that layer comes in, or when the target is spawned
+later. A legacy area resolved its links exactly once and never recovered.
+
+**A closed spline in Parallel Transport mode can show one twist at its seam.** That is inherent to a
+rotation-minimizing frame: it is not generally periodic, so the frame that comes back around does
+not have to match the one that left. Legacy Up has no seam and flips near vertical instead. Pick
+whichever artefact is out of shot.
+
+**One shape per entity.** Use child entities when you need two.
+
+---
+
+## Console
+
+| Command | What it does |
+| --- | --- |
+| `area_SplineSelfTest` | Walks the spline maths through every degenerate case a create gesture can leave behind — 1 point, 2 coincident points, 2 points, 3 collinear, 3 vertical, 3 general, open and closed, 0–2 smoothing passes, both frame modes — and reports the first non-finite position, tangent, handle, length or normal. |
+| `es_DrawAreas 1` | Engine command: draw every area and its fade band. `es_DrawAreaDebug 1` adds the text overlay, `2` logs enter/leave transitions. |
+
+---
+
+## Known limits
+
+- **Box per-face sound obstruction is not authored.** The legacy AreaBox has six checkboxes; the box
+  shape has no members for them yet, so its six side flags are all off.
+- **Bake To Brushes keeps no render flags and no grouping** — baked brushes get a hand-placed
+  brush's defaults, and are named rather than grouped.
+- **No curves over distance.** The distributor's scale ramp is two numbers (**Scale Ramp Start** /
+  **Scale Ramp End**) rather than an editable curve, because the inspector has nowhere to host a
+  curve editor yet. A curve is a drop-in replacement when it does.
+- **No Solid shape kind.** There is no producer of convex hulls outside the level-geometry tools, so
+  a solid area has no component form.
+- **An entity that already carries a legacy area proxy stays inert**: the Area component refuses to
+  adopt it and warns once, because adopting it would silently stop that entity's `<Area>` node being
+  saved. Build new areas on new entities.
+- **No per-vertex height** on the polygon (the roof is flat at *lowest Z + Height*), no filled
+  display, and no merge / split / reverse tools for polygon or spline.
+- **No road and no river** function components yet.
+- A polygon's roof ring is drawn and picked along **world** up, so a rotated entity extrudes visually
+  along world Z. Legacy does exactly the same; this is parity, not a regression.
+- Objects created through **Create Object → Area** persist their class name in the level, so a level
+  containing them needs this editor plugin present to load.
